      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmill damage in transit is not done by the truck. It is done by a folded deck
          that was never strapped, a console cable that was pulled tight when the uprights came
          off, a bag of bolts that went into a different box, or a carton that was never designed
          to be opened and closed twice. Treadmill packing is not difficult, but it rewards doing
          things in the right order. This guide covers packing for a house move, for storage and
          for a return to the seller, and links to our separate guides for the physical jobs of
          moving and dismantling the machine rather than repeating them.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Pack a Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>To pack a treadmill, unplug it, lower the incline, remove the safety key and
            loose accessories, lock and strap the deck (or remove the console and uprights), bag and
            label every bolt you take out, wrap the console separately, then box or blanket-wrap the
            machine so that nothing can shift.</strong> The original box is the best container if
            you still have it, but a treadmill can be packed perfectly well without it using moving
            blankets, stretch wrap, corner protection and straps.
          </p>
          <p>
            How far you go depends on why you are packing. A local move in a van needs far less
            than a return to the manufacturer by freight, and storage for a year needs different
            attention again, mostly to damp and dust. The sections below take each situation in
            turn. The physical work of getting the machine out of the house and down the stairs is
            covered in our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to move a treadmill</a>,
            and the work of taking it apart is covered step by step in
            <a href="/how-to-disassemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to disassemble a treadmill</a>.
            This page is about what happens between those two: protecting the parts and keeping
            them together.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing to settle before anything else is the weight of the packed machine. A
            folding home treadmill commonly weighs somewhere in the region of 150 to 250 lb before
            any packaging, which already puts it over what the parcel services will take as a
            single package. That single fact decides most of the choices later on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-are-you-packing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Why Are You Packing It? Moving, Storage and Returns Differ</h2>
<p>
            The right level of treadmill packing depends on who will handle the machine and for how
            long. It is worth deciding which of three situations you are in, because it changes
            what matters.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A house move you control</h3>
          <p>
            If you or movers you have booked are loading the treadmill into a van and unloading it
            a few hours later, the job is protection against knocks and against the deck dropping.
            The machine stays upright or flat the whole time, nobody throws it, and you can see it.
            Blankets, straps and a well-bagged set of hardware are usually enough, and the original
            box is a bonus rather than a necessity.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Storage</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill going into a storage unit, a garage or a basement for months needs
            protection from damp, dust and pests more than from impact. Electronics and a
            fibreboard deck do not like humidity, and a sealed plastic wrap that traps moisture
            against the frame can do more harm than a breathable cover. The packing for storage
            section below covers what to do differently.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A return or a shipment by carrier</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill going back to the seller, or being shipped to a buyer, passes through hands
            you will never see. It may be lifted by a forklift, stood on end, stacked against other
            freight and moved several times. Here the packaging has to do all the work on its own,
            and the seller's terms may dictate exactly what that packaging is. This is where the
            original box matters most, and where reading the return policy before you start can
            save a refund deduction. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            covers fees, pickups and windows; this page covers the packing itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Should You Do Before Packing Anything?</h2>
<p>
            Before any wrapping, put the machine into a safe, compact state and record how it was
            set up. Ten minutes here prevents most of the problems that show up on the other side.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lower the incline to zero while the machine is still powered.</strong> A
            power-incline treadmill packed at an incline is taller, more top-heavy and puts strain on
            the incline motor and its linkage. Run it all the way down, then switch off.</li>
            <li><strong>Unplug at the wall and remove the safety key.</strong> Put the key in a
            small sealed bag. It is the single most commonly lost part after a move, and without it
            most treadmills will not start.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the power cord if it detaches.</strong> Many treadmills have a cord
            that unplugs from the machine. Coil it loosely, tie it with a soft tie rather than a
            tight knot, and pack it with the safety key. If the cord is fixed, coil it and tape or
            tie it against the frame so it cannot trail under a wheel or snag on a doorway.</li>
            <li><strong>Take off everything that is not part of the frame.</strong> Tablet holders,
            bottle cages, accessory trays, clip-on fans, chest straps, the mat. Anything left on
            will rattle loose in transit or scratch the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Clean and dry it.</strong> Wipe the belt, rails and console. Sweat and dust
            packed into a box for weeks are not good for either the electronics or the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Photograph everything.</strong> The console from the front and back, the
            wiring where it enters the upright, every bolt you are about to remove, and the serial
            number sticker on the frame. These photographs are your reassembly guide and, for a
            return, your evidence of the condition it left in.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the manual.</strong> If you have lost it, most manufacturers publish
            manuals online by model number. It tells you whether the deck locks, whether the
            machine may be laid down, and in what order the console comes off.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are returning the machine, also check whether the seller wants it disassembled at
            all. Some do not, as the section on returns below explains, and dismantling a machine
            that the carrier would have collected assembled is wasted effort with some risk
            attached.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="original-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Should You Use the Original Box?</h2>
<p>
            If you still have the original box and its inserts, use them: they were designed for
            this machine and this weight, and some sellers require them for returns. The box is
            not magic, though, and a damaged or partly recycled box can be worse than good
            improvised packing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What makes the original packaging valuable</strong> is not the cardboard, which
            is often single-use quality, but the inserts. Treadmill boxes usually include moulded
            foam or cardboard end caps that hold the frame off the walls of the carton, blocks that
            stop the folded deck moving, and a compartment or bag for the hardware. Those parts
            position the heavy motor end where the carton is strongest. If you kept the end caps
            and lost the outer box, you can often build a new outer layer around them with
            cardboard sheet and stretch wrap.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When the original box is not good enough.</strong> A box that was cut open
            along its sides during unpacking, has been crushed in a garage, or has got damp, has
            lost much of its strength. Corrugated cardboard that has absorbed moisture can lose a
            large part of its stacking strength, and a treadmill box needs that strength because
            freight is often stacked. If the box is soft, torn at the corners or missing its
            inserts, treat it as a dust cover and add your own protection inside and out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repacking it the way it came.</strong> Machines are usually packed partly
            assembled: the base with the deck, the uprights and console in a separate position or
            box, and hardware in a bag. If you have photographs from the day it arrived, or the
            assembly section of the manual, follow the same layout in reverse. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to assemble a treadmill</a>
            describes the usual packed state and the console cable, which is the part most easily
            damaged when the process is reversed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the box for the return window anyway.</strong> If you have just bought a
            treadmill, keep the flattened box and every insert somewhere dry until the return
            window has closed. It costs a corner of a garage and can save a deduction or a
            dispute. After that, keep it if you expect to move house in the next few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="securing-the-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Do You Secure the Deck?</h2>
<p>
            The deck is the heaviest moving part and the most likely to cause damage, so it is
            secured first: folded and locked with a strap added on a folding machine, or left flat
            and protected on a non-folding one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding treadmills.</strong> Raise the deck until the latch engages and check
            it has fully seated. If the machine has a secondary lock pin or knob, fit it. Then add
            your own restraint: a ratchet strap or two heavy-duty straps around the folded deck and
            the upright frame, tight enough to stop movement but not so tight that they bend the
            side rails. A gas strut slows the deck, it does not hold it, and a latch is designed to
            hold the deck while the machine stands still on a level floor, not while it is tilted
            on a dolly or lying in a van. The strap is what stops the deck dropping if the latch
            lets go. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-fold-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to fold a treadmill</a>
            explains the different latch types. If the latch is worn or will not hold, do not pack
            it folded; lower the deck and pack it as a non-folding machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Non-folding treadmills.</strong> The deck stays flat. Protect the belt surface
            with a sheet of cardboard or a blanket so that nothing stacked against it can scuff it,
            and protect the side rails, which are often plastic-capped and stick out further than
            anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt.</strong> There is no need to slacken the belt for a move or storage,
            and doing so means re-tensioning and re-tracking it afterwards. Leave it as it is. If
            you have the machine apart for a long storage period, check the tension and tracking
            when it is set up again, as you would after any move.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Protect the rear roller end caps.</strong> The rear of the deck often carries
            plastic end caps covering the roller adjustment bolts. They are easily cracked when the
            machine is set down on its end or rolled on its transport wheels. A folded towel taped
            over each end costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-uprights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Do You Protect the Console and Uprights?</h2>
<p>
            The console is the most fragile and most expensive part to replace, so either wrap it
            in place with the machine folded or remove it and pack it separately in its own padded
            box. The choice depends on how far the machine is travelling and who is handling it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leaving the console on.</strong> For a local move, most folding treadmills can
            travel with the console attached. Wrap the console in a blanket or bubble wrap, cover
            any touchscreen with a sheet of cardboard first so that pressure is spread rather than
            concentrated, and tape or stretch-wrap the padding in place without taping directly to
            the screen or the plastic. Fold the handrails in if the design allows.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Taking the console and uprights off.</strong> For freight, for a return that
            requires disassembly, for storage of more than a few weeks, or for a large machine that
            will not fit through a doorway assembled, remove the console and uprights. The
            procedure is in
            <a href="/how-to-disassemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our disassembly guide</a>;
            what matters for packing is the console cable. That cable runs up the inside of one
            upright and is joined by a connector, usually near the base. It must be disconnected,
            not pulled, and the free ends must be protected. Put a small bag over each connector,
            secure it with a soft tie, and tape the cable so it cannot be pinched when the upright
            is laid down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Packing the removed console.</strong> Wrap it in bubble wrap or foam, screen
            side protected with cardboard, and put it in a box on its own with packing paper or
            foam filling every gap so it cannot move when the box is shaken. Mark the box as
            fragile and which way is up. Do not pack the console in the same box as the uprights
            or the hardware bag; a loose bag of bolts against a screen will crack it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Uprights and handrails.</strong> These are tough steel tubes, but their painted
            finish and plastic trims mark easily. Wrap them in a blanket or cardboard, tape the
            pair together, and pack them alongside the base where they cannot slide into the
            console box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hardware-and-labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Should You Bag and Label the Hardware?</h2>
<p>
            Bag the bolts from each joint separately, label each bag with where it came from, and
            attach the bags to the machine itself rather than packing them in a general box. Lost
            hardware is the most common reason a treadmill cannot be reassembled on the day it
            arrives.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>One bag per joint.</strong> Treadmill bolts often look alike and are not.
            The bolts that hold the uprights to the base may be a different length from those that
            hold the console, and fitting a long bolt where a short one belongs can foul a cable or
            a moving part. Use small zip-lock bags and keep each set separate.</li>
            <li><strong>Label clearly.</strong> Write on each bag what it holds and where it goes:
            "left upright to base, 4 bolts, 4 washers". Matching the label to a photograph number
            makes reassembly quick.</li>
            <li><strong>Tape the bags to the frame.</strong> Tape them inside the base or to the
            underside of the motor hood area where they will not be crushed, or put all the bags in
            one strong bag and tie that to the frame. The aim is that the hardware cannot be
            separated from the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Pack the tools.</strong> If the machine came with Allen keys and a spanner,
            put them in with the hardware. They are the right sizes and they will not be in a
            toolbox at the other end.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety key and power cord.</strong> In their own sealed bag, attached
            to the console box or the base, and listed on the outside of the package.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a move, also label the parts themselves: a strip of masking tape on each upright
            marked left or right, and on the end of the console cable. For a return, check whether
            the seller supplies a parts checklist; if so, tick it off and photograph it with the
            packed hardware.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="without-the-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How Do You Pack a Treadmill Without the Original Box?</h2>
<p>
            Without the original box, treadmill packaging is built from moving blankets, stretch
            wrap, cardboard corner and edge protection, and straps, with the console packed
            separately in a strong box. For a carrier shipment, the whole machine usually also goes
            on a pallet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What you need</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Two or three heavy moving blankets, or furniture pads</li>
            <li>A roll of stretch wrap (pallet wrap), which holds blankets in place without tape
            touching the machine</li>
            <li>Cardboard sheet and cardboard corner or edge protectors</li>
            <li>Two ratchet straps or heavy-duty cam straps</li>
            <li>Bubble wrap or foam for the console, and a strong double-walled box for it</li>
            <li>Small zip-lock bags, a marker and masking tape for the hardware</li>
            <li>For freight: a pallet that is wider and longer than the machine's footprint</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The order of work</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Prepare and secure</strong> the machine as described above: incline down,
            unplugged, key removed, deck locked and strapped or lowered, console removed or
            padded.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect the corners and edges.</strong> Fit cardboard corners to the ends of
            the base and the rear of the deck, and cardboard sheet over the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Blanket the machine.</strong> Drape blankets over the whole frame, with the
            motor hood and the console area getting the thickest layer.</li>
            <li><strong>Stretch-wrap the blankets in place.</strong> Wrap around the blankets, not
            the bare machine, so that the film does not mark the paint or pull off labels.</li>
            <li><strong>Strap it.</strong> Straps go around the frame at its strongest points, not
            around the console or across the motor hood.</li>
            <li><strong>Mark it.</strong> Write "this way up" and "heavy end" on the wrap at the
            motor end, and attach a label with the destination address and a phone number if a
            carrier is handling it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            <strong>Building a box from scratch is rarely worth it.</strong> Large appliance boxes
            and custom-cut crates exist, and shipping stores will build one for a fee, but for a
            treadmill on a pallet, blankets and stretch wrap with good corner protection do the
            same job. The exception is a seller who specifically requires a box, in which case ask
            whether they will supply one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="freight-vs-parcel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Freight or Parcel: How Will a Packed Treadmill Travel?</h2>
<p>
            Almost every full-size treadmill travels as freight rather than as a parcel, because
            the two largest US parcel carriers cap a single package at 150 lb. On their own
            published limits, checked on 14 October 2026, both UPS and FedEx Ground accept
            packages up to 150 lb and up to 165 inches in length plus girth, with a maximum length
            of 108 inches. A folding treadmill that weighs 150 lb or more before packaging is over
            that limit before the box goes on.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Machine</th>
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Typical weight before packing</th>
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Usually ships as</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Walking pad</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Roughly 45 to 75 lb</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Parcel, if within size limits</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Folding home treadmill</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Roughly 150 to 250 lb</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Freight (LTL), usually on a pallet</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Non-folding home treadmill</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Roughly 250 to 350 lb</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Freight (LTL) on a pallet</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Commercial treadmill</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Roughly 350 to 500 lb or more</td>
                  <td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Freight, often with a liftgate</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The weights are typical ranges, not figures for any particular model; our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill weighs</a>
            explains where they come from. Check your own model's shipping weight in the manual or
            on the original box label.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What freight means for packing.</strong> Less-than-truckload freight moves on
            pallets, by forklift, through terminals where it is loaded alongside other shipments.
            The packed treadmill should be strapped or wrapped to a pallet so that it cannot slide
            off, with nothing overhanging the edges, and marked so that nothing heavy is stacked on
            it. Ask whether the carrier offers a liftgate and residential pickup, because a pallet
            weighing several hundred pounds does not come down a driveway by hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Splitting it into parcels.</strong> It is sometimes possible to dismantle a
            treadmill far enough to ship the parts as several parcels under 150 lb each. That
            usually means removing the motor or splitting the frame, which is well beyond normal
            disassembly, voids most warranties, and leaves the heaviest box still close to the
            limit. For a full-size machine it is rarely worth it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Insurance.</strong> Freight and parcel liability limits are usually far lower
            than the value of a treadmill unless additional coverage is bought. If you are shipping
            a machine you have sold, declare its value and photograph it packed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="packing-for-a-return" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Do Returns Require?</h2>
<p>
            Return requirements vary by seller, so read the policy before you pack: some require the
            original packaging and full disassembly, while others will collect the machine
            unboxed. Packing it the wrong way can cost you money either way.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sellers that collect unboxed.</strong> Some manufacturers selling direct
            arrange their own pickup and wrap the machine themselves. Sole's published return terms
            for direct purchases, checked on its own site on 14 October 2026, state that it does
            not require cardio equipment to be boxed before pickup, that the unit can be assembled
            or disassembled, and that it must be placed in an accessible location for collection.
            The same terms deduct a return shipping fee and a restocking fee from the refund, and
            apply only to purchases made directly from the brand; machines bought from a retailer
            follow the retailer's policy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sellers that require boxing.</strong> Other sellers, including many general
            retailers and marketplaces, expect the treadmill to come back disassembled and packed,
            sometimes in the original packaging specifically. Where that is the requirement,
            returning a machine in improvised packaging can lead to a refusal or a deduction for
            damage. If you no longer have the box, ask before you start whether the seller can
            supply one or accepts equivalent protection, and get the answer in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Things to check in the policy before packing:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Does the machine need to be boxed, and in the original packaging?</li>
            <li>Should it be assembled or disassembled for collection?</li>
            <li>Is the pickup curbside, or will the carrier come into the home?</li>
            <li>Which parts and accessories must be included, such as the safety key, the cord,
            manuals and tools?</li>
            <li>Are there deductions for missing parts or for damage found at inspection?</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Document the condition.</strong> Photograph the machine assembled and working,
            then each stage of packing, the hardware bags laid out, and the packed result next to
            the collection paperwork. If the refund is disputed, this is your evidence. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">return policy guide</a>
            covers restocking fees and pickup charges in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="packing-for-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. How Should a Treadmill Be Packed for Storage?</h2>
<p>
            For storage, pack the treadmill to keep it dry, clean and stable rather than to survive
            impact: a breathable cover over a clean machine, raised off a concrete floor, in a space
            that does not swing between damp and dry.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Choose the space carefully.</strong> A climate-controlled unit or an
            indoor room is far better than an unheated garage or a damp basement. Humidity swells
            fibreboard decks and lifts their laminate, and condensation is bad for the control
            board. If a garage is the only option, a dehumidifier helps. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-water-damage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill water damage</a>
            explains what moisture does to the electronics and the deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not seal it in plastic.</strong> Stretch wrap is fine for a move of a
            few days. For months it traps moisture against the frame and the motor. Use a fabric
            cover, a cotton dust sheet or blankets that let air through.</li>
            <li><strong>Lift it off the floor.</strong> A pallet or a couple of timber battens
            under the base keeps it out of any water on the floor and away from the cold that
            draws condensation.</li>
            <li><strong>Store it in its normal orientation.</strong> Standing on its feet, flat or
            folded as designed, not on its side or end. Leaning a treadmill against a wall on its
            end for months loads the motor mounts and the console in ways they were not designed
            for.</li>
            <li><strong>Release strap tension.</strong> Straps that were tight for the journey can
            be loosened to a snug hold for storage, so they do not slowly distort side rails or
            plastic trims.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the console and the hardware dry.</strong> If the console has been
            removed, store it indoors if possible, with the hardware and key bag taped to its
            box.</li>
            <li><strong>Deter pests.</strong> Mice like the inside of motor hoods. Keep the
            storage space clean and do not store the treadmill next to anything that attracts
            them.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            When the treadmill comes out of storage, check it before using it: look for damp or
            corrosion on the deck and the electronics, lubricate the deck if the manual calls for
            it, check belt tension and tracking, and run it slowly with nobody on it before you
            step on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="unpacking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. What Should You Check When You Unpack It?</h2>
<p>
            When the treadmill arrives, inspect it before you sign for it if a carrier delivered
            it, then reassemble it from your photographs and labels and test it slowly before
            anyone uses it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>At delivery.</strong> If a carrier brings it, look at the packaging for
            crushed corners, punctures or obvious damage while the driver is there, and note any
            damage on the delivery receipt before signing. A clean signature is hard to dispute
            later. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            covers the inspection in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reassembly.</strong> Use the bags in the order you labelled them and your
            photographs of the console wiring. Route the console cable exactly as it was, with no
            pinch points where the upright meets the base. Do not force a bolt that will not start
            by hand; it is the wrong bolt or a crossed thread.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Before the first session.</strong> Plug it into a suitable outlet, insert the
            safety key, and run the belt at the lowest speed with nobody on it. Check that it runs
            centred, that the incline moves through its range, and that pulling the safety key
            stops the belt. Then walk on it at a slow pace, holding the rails, before using it
            normally. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a>
            lists the full set of after-move checks.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need the original box to pack a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for a move or storage. Moving blankets, stretch wrap, corner protection and straps protect a treadmill well, with the console packed separately in a strong box. The original box matters most for returns, because some sellers require it. Check the return policy first, and ask whether the seller supplies packaging if you no longer have it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill be shipped by UPS or FedEx?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A full-size treadmill usually cannot go as a single parcel, because UPS and FedEx Ground both cap a package at 150 lb, and most folding treadmills weigh about that before packaging. They normally travel as freight on a pallet. A small walking pad can often go as a parcel if it is within the size limits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a treadmill be packed folded or flat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A folding treadmill can be packed folded if the latch engages fully and you add a strap around the deck and frame, because the latch alone is not designed to hold the deck in transit. A non-folding treadmill is packed flat on its feet. Avoid packing or storing either kind on its side or end unless the manual allows it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to remove the console to pack a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. For a local move the console can often stay on, wrapped in a blanket with a cardboard sheet over the screen. For freight, a return that requires disassembly, long storage or a tight doorway, remove it, disconnect the console cable at its connector rather than pulling it, and pack the console in its own padded box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep track of treadmill bolts when packing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bag the bolts from each joint separately in small zip-lock bags, write on each bag what it holds and where it goes, and tape the bags to the frame so they cannot be separated from the machine. Photograph each joint before you take it apart, and pack the supplied Allen keys and spanner with the hardware.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I loosen the treadmill belt before packing it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no need for a normal move or storage. Leaving the belt as it is avoids having to re-tension and re-track it afterwards. Protect the belt surface with cardboard or a blanket so nothing scuffs it. After any move, check the tension and tracking at a slow speed before using the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Packing a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most transit damage to treadmills comes from a handful of avoidable packing errors rather than from rough handling.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the fold latch alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A fold latch is designed to hold the deck while the machine stands still on a level floor, and a gas strut only slows the deck. Tilted on a dolly, bumped on a step or lying in a van, either can let go. Always add a strap around the folded deck and the frame.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pulling the console cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The console cable runs inside an upright and joins at a connector. Lifting the console off without disconnecting it, or laying an upright down on the cable, damages the wires or the plug, and the console will not work at the other end. Disconnect it at the connector and protect both ends.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing the hardware in a general box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bolts packed in a box of miscellaneous items get lost, mixed up or delivered to a different room. Bag each set separately, label it with where it goes, and tape the bags to the treadmill itself, along with the safety key and power cord.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing it in plastic for storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Stretch wrap is fine for a few days in a van, but months of it trap moisture against the frame, the motor and the deck. For storage, use a breathable cover, raise the machine off a concrete floor and choose a dry space.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

